# ChangeLog

## Unreleased

 - Scans are now sandboxed by default. Landlock confines the run to the kernel tree (read-only),
   the rules and a temp directory with no network, and a seccomp filter closes what Landlock
   structurally cannot express -- UDP and netlink sockets, io_uring, ptrace, namespace syscalls.
   Both are installed once just before the worker pool, so they cover the workers, every spatch,
   and the /bin/sh, git, find, rm and diff that spatch shells out to. --sandbox=off skips it,
   --sandbox=strict refuses to scan when the kernel cannot provide it, and the default falls back
   to an unconfined scan on a kernel without Landlock. A self-test runs after the lock: an
   over-tight policy would otherwise show up as "0 files match" rather than as an error
 - spatch now runs with GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL=/dev/null and GIT_CONFIG_NOSYSTEM=1. Handed a directory
   it prefilters by shelling out to `git grep`, and that git was reading the user's ~/.gitconfig,
   so a setting there could change what a scan found on one machine but not another. cvehound
   supplies safe.directory for the scanned tree over GIT_CONFIG_* (the "command" scope, which git
   treats as protected), so a checkout owned by another user still scans -- previously that needed
   the user's own git config, and without it git refused the repository and spatch reported
   "0 files match" with exit 0

## 1.5.0 - Aug 27 2026

 - spatch runs are now bounded by two time budgets instead of none: SPATCH_TIMEOUT (CPU-seconds,
   via spatch's own --timeout) and SPATCH_WALL_TIMEOUT (a wall-clock watchdog covering the rule
   parsing and I/O that --timeout can't see). A rule that fires either budget now fails the CVE and
   is recorded in report['errors'], instead of silently returning partial or no results
 - validate-rule.sh (the write-cve-rule skill) now fails a rule that would blow the same matching
   budget cvehound enforces at runtime, and checks grep-prefilter selectivity: a rule with no or
   overly common literal tokens makes spatch parse the whole tree, which used to go undetected until
   a whole-tree scan actually paid for it
 - CVE-2020-12352 collapsed from ten near-identical starred rules into the one invariant they all
   repeat. Past Coccinelle's max_cnf = 5, CNF conversion gives up and drops the grep query entirely,
   so the old rule parsed the entire kernel tree (650s CPU) for a function that no longer exists
   upstream; the collapsed rule scans in 0.2s with unchanged verdicts
 - Five more rules (CVE-2019-15925, CVE-2022-0492, CVE-2017-7558, CVE-2020-11884, CVE-2023-50431)
   stopped leaking common tokens into their grep query, cutting whole-tree parse costs by one to two
   orders of magnitude with no change in verdicts
 - Fixed content-latest publishing: the push trigger watched master, which this repo stopped using,
   so every rule push since had been a no-op — the rolling content set still served pre-conversion
   (script:python) rules that the bundled python-less spatch refuses to run. Now watches main
 - docs/WRITING_RULES.md gained Rule 8 (keep the grep query selective), documenting how to measure
   and avoid this class of cost, and documents exactly what the two spatch time budgets cover and
   don't
 - --runslow is substantially faster and more reliable: whole-tree verdicts are now memoized,
   worktree scans spread across every worker instead of one branch per group, metadata lookups batch
   through one long-lived git process instead of forking one per entry, and spatch's GC is tuned for
   its actual allocation pattern. CI can dispatch a slow run against a named subset of rules, and the
   missing_backports allowlist now fails once a listed backport actually lands instead of silently
   XPASSing forever

## 1.4.0 - Aug 25 2026

 - Added an optional bundled coccinelle: `pip install 'cvehound[spatch]'`
   pulls a prebuilt, tailored spatch (the cvehound-spatch wheel, Linux
   x86_64 and aarch64), so scanning no longer requires coccinelle on the
   system
 - The spatch binary is now resolved explicitly: `--spatch` (or a `spatch`
   key in the config file), then $CVEHOUND_SPATCH, then the bundled
   cvehound-spatch package, then PATH. A binary named explicitly is an error
   when it does not resolve instead of a silent fallback, and reports record
   which binary produced the findings
 - Converted all 534 detection rules from `@script:python` reporting to `*`
   context mode, and cvehound now parses spatch's diff instead of grepping
   its python output. Rules no longer need a python-enabled spatch, and a
   detection and the locations it reports can no longer disagree
 - CVE-2021-3411 is now a grep rule. CVE-2016-5195, CVE-2017-1000112,
   CVE-2019-14821 and CVE-2021-28971 are anchored on the enclosing function
   definition, which is orders of magnitude faster than the previous spelling
 - Fixed a CVE-2023-42753 false positive on linux-next, CVE-2021-28971 and
   CVE-2021-3656 `Files:` coverage, and CVE-2022-1016 guarding on a file
   other than the one it detects in; rules whose range spans a rename now
   list the pre-rename paths too
 - A missing diff(1) is now an error: spatch renders what it matched by
   shelling out to diff and otherwise reports nothing while still exiting
   successfully. When spatch does fail, cvehound reports what it said
 - Fixed `python -m cvehound` crashing on Python 3.14 and macOS, where the
   pool callables were unpicklable under the forkserver/spawn start methods
 - Reworked out-of-band content updates: `cvehound update` downloads a
   checksummed, versioned content set (rules + metadata) from the rolling
   content-latest GitHub release and installs it atomically under
   ~/.local/share/cvehound/ — the installed package is never modified. The
   old updater could destroy the installed rules on a failed download
 - Removed the `cvehound_update_rules` script; `cvehound update` replaces
   it. An invalid or too-new content set falls back to the packaged baseline
 - Removed kernel_cves.json.gz from git: a weekly CI job regenerates it and
   publishes it as a content-latest asset; the PyPI build copies it into the
   package. `cvehound_update_metadata` is a maintainer/CI tool now, writes
   atomically, refuses empty or drastically shrunken results, and defaults
   its output to the working directory
 - `--version` now reports the rules and metadata identity, JSON reports
   record which content produced the findings (`tools.rules`,
   `tools.metadata`) plus the previously missing args (metadata, exclude,
   exploit, ignore_files), and stale metadata prints a warning (--exploit
   without metadata is an error since it would silently match nothing)
 - Validated $CVEHOUND_METADATA like --metadata; non-rule files in the rules
   dir are ignored and the disputed group no longer depends on the install
   path; an unparsable rule Version: header no longer aborts the whole scan
 - Dropped support for coccinelle < 1.1.0 (last tested in CI long ago); a
   warning is printed when an older spatch is detected
 - Documented the scriptless context-mode rule idiom in docs/WRITING_RULES.md,
   rewrote the contrib templates around it, and taught the rule validator to
   check the old end of a rule's commit range, where a `Files:` path that
   resolves nowhere used to make the rule silently match nothing
 - Rewrote the test suite to check rules against per-commit trees materialized
   from git blobs, in parallel and with cached verdicts, instead of checking
   the kernel tree out for every commit

## 1.3.0 - Aug 18 2026

 - Added detection rules for 16 new CVE IDs and updated 25 existing rules
 - Moved upstream-rejected CVEs to the disputed rules group
 - Fixed Kbuild parsing on kernels >= 5.14: arch/, io_uring/, rust/ and virt/kvm
   files are mapped to CONFIG_ options again (seeding from the root Kbuild, top
   Makefile and arch Makefile; $(srctree)/$(SRCARCH) resolution)
 - Added --arch option with ARCH -> SRCARCH normalization
 - CONFIG_ symbols absent from the .config are now treated as disabled
 - Files of another architecture are reported as not affected (--check-strict
   drops them); files unknown to the Kbuild parser are reported with a warning
   and marked mapped=false in the report
 - --check-strict without an existing .config is now an error instead of
   silently dropping results
 - Documented --check-strict
 - Reduced worker IPC: the CVEhound instance is sent to each worker once
 - Replaced the deprecated CVE metadata source with kernel.org vulns.git and
   CIP kernel-sec, and switched exploit status to CISA KEV
 - Linked CVE records to cve.org and removed CWE and CVSS fields from reports
 - Hardened CVE rule execution and improved metadata and condition-evaluation
   performance
 - Raised the minimum Python version to 3.11
 - Migrated packaging and development tooling to uv, Ruff and ty; replaced the
   lxml runtime dependency with PyYAML

